!TP_ICM_WORKING_PRIMFRAME_TOOLTIP;You have three options for modifying the primaries by selecting them from the 'Destination primaries' combo box:\n1) By selecting 'Custom (Linear sliders)' and changing the linear coordinates of Red, Green, and Blue (Rx,  Ry,  Gx,  Gy,  Bx,  By). In this case: a) the graphical representation of the CIExy diagram is updated; b) the polar coordinates 'Hue rotation: R.rot, G.rot, B.rot' and 'Saturation (with reference to the distance to the White point): R.sat, G.sat, B.sat' remain at zero. The system calculates the new X, Y, and Z values, taking into account the actual White Point.\n\n2) By directly changing the values using the CIExy diagram. When 'Custom CIExy diagram' is selected in 'Destination primaries', you can modify the values of the three primaries directly on the graph. The system calculates the new X, Y, and Z values. Note that in this case, the White Point position on the graph will not be updated. The polar coordinates (R.rot,  R.sat,  G.rot,  G.sat,  B.rot,  B.sat) remain at zero. Despite the small size of this CIExy diagram, it is probably the best way to master the system. Three small fixed dots represent the positions of the Rec2020 primaries, the default 'Working profile'. This allows you to see the adjustments you are making.\n\n3) With the 'Custom' (Polar sliders)  the polar coordinates 'Hue rotation: R.rot, G.rot, B.rot' and 'Saturation (with reference to the distance to the White point): R.sat, G.sat, B.sat': a) the graphical representation of the CIExy diagram is updated; b) the linear coordinates (Rx, Ry, Gx, Gy, Bx, By) are not updated. The system calculates the new X, Y, and Z values. After selecting polar coordinates: a) if you change the combo box to 'Custom (CIE xy Diagram)', the linear coordinate values will be updated. The polar coordinates (R.rot, R.sat, G.rot, G.sat, B.rot, B.sat) will remain at zero; b) if you change the combo box to 'Custom (Linear sliders)', the system will revert to the default values.\n\nBe very careful when using this type of tool - whether it's polar sliders, linear sliders, or CIExy graphs. If you go outside the CIExy diagram, you'll generate imaginary colors, how will they be handled? If you reduce the primary values too much, you risk out-of-gamut data. Use with caution, and with small values, while monitoring the primaries' deviation from the CIExy diagram.
